{:A {:score 8
     :reason "Most creature lines come from real chores: \"It's my five o'clock worm.\" matches Flora's 17:00 feed, \"Seven o'clock hay round?\" matches Juniper's 07:00/19:00 hay, and \"Leash is right there.\" matches the leash by the door. The memorial hal-note \"I'm sorry about {name}. Their chores are off your list; the day no longer waits on them.\" is warm and truthful to §2.5. Points come off for two :fed lines that promise a fixed interval the clock-anchored schedule doesn't keep, and for one hal-note that reads like a status change rather than a companion."
     :violations [{:where ":juniper :fed \"See you in twelve hours.\""
                   :rule 3
                   :issue "The hay chore is anchored at 07:00 with :every \"12h\". If the player hays late, the next one is due sooner than twelve hours, so the line can state something untrue (request note 6, pillar 'real, not simulated')."
                   :fix "\"Full. That'll hold me a while.\""}
                  {:where ":flora :fed \"Two days till the next one.\""
                   :rule 3
                   :issue "Flora's feed is anchored at 17:00 every 2d. After a late feed the next one is less than two days away, so the bubble can misreport the real schedule."
                   :fix "\"Full. Back to the weeds for a bit.\""}
                  {:where ":memorial :hal-note \"{name}'s in memorial. No tasks, no tending — just a place for them in the sun.\""
                   :rule 1
                   :issue "\"{name}'s in memorial. No tasks\" reads as a system status change, not the twin talking to you as a companion. It also nearly repeats hal-note #1."
                   :fix "\"{name}'s chores are off your list now. The sunny spot stays theirs.\""}]}
 :B {:score 9
     :reason "The :hungry lines stay one steady, unescalating ask, as the §2.3 pressure ceiling requires (\"Worms, please?\", \"Hay time, no?\"). None of them carries a \"!\", so the declared needs-you mark can lead the bubble. Biscuit's lines cover only his walk, his own chore, and never his feeding, which is :who :spouse. One :hungry line implies a rising state."
     :violations [{:where ":juniper :hungry \"Getting hungry.\""
                   :rule 6
                   :issue "\"Getting\" implies hunger that keeps rising. An overdue chore is one steady state that never escalates (§2.3 pressure ceiling, request note 6), and this line still says \"getting\" hours into the overdue state."
                   :fix "\"Hungry. Hay when you can.\""}]}
 :C {:score 10
